WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.000
[Scale Swap Challenge:]

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:04.907
[We asked three imaging specialists to swap scales and share their results.]

00:00:04.907 --> 00:00:07.126
[#1: Photo to Micro]

00:00:07.126 --> 00:00:08.320
[Keith Ellenbogen, Wildlife Photographer]

00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:09.680
My name is Keith Ellenbogen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:15.040
I'm a wildlife photographer and I really&nbsp;
focus mostly on underwater environments.

00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:20.160
I really enjoyed the challenge here of looking not&nbsp;
only just at big animals but now to look at these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:24.560
microscopic, planktonic creatures. So I&nbsp;
went about and collected some plankton&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.520 --> 00:00:31.840
and put them in a bowl. And here you can see a&nbsp;
concentrated amount of plankton that I sampled,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:37.200
and then I took a little bit of this and&nbsp;
photographed it under a light microscope&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:42.000
using dark-field. And what I really enjoyed about&nbsp;
this is it's really the same skills I have. A lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:47.200
of patience, a lot of looking around, a lot of&nbsp;
finding the right animal and then photographing.

00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:51.280
And this is what's called the novotny&nbsp;
which I looked up, it's cool, little.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:54.400
I sort of like them because they have this&nbsp;
cone head kind of shape and you can see that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.120 --> 00:00:59.440
what is different a little bit from a microscope&nbsp;
in some ways, depth of field which in photography&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:04.560
is always omnipresent but it's much more present&nbsp;
in the microscope. And here one on the top is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:08.880
sort of translucent and they just floated on the&nbsp;
surface they're really gorgeous, and you can see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:12.960
inside it you can see it's pregnant with a&nbsp;
number of them, and they bloom really fast, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:17.600
I was learning about them, and you can see just&nbsp;
they're stacked one right on top of the other,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:23.360
under a slide within a smallest drop of water. I&nbsp;
just I really love the the feeling of this this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:29.840
image and and the discovery of this animal. This&nbsp;
is a dinoflagellate, it's a photosynthesis so it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:35.360
takes the sun and makes a lot of the oxygen we&nbsp;
breathe. They're really spectacular they're very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.360 --> 00:01:40.320
present in the in the waters there they're the&nbsp;
most common one that I that I saw. And I liked it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:45.280
because in this one, you know, it sort of moved a&nbsp;
little bit and I liked a little bit of the motion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:50.560
that happened and I stacked the images, I took a&nbsp;
couple of these and brought them together so that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:55.280
unlike the other one that the depth of field was&nbsp;
sort of merged together, and I liked playing with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.280 --> 00:02:00.400
this. I really found myself enjoying looking under&nbsp;
a microscope in a way that I've never really done.

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:05.840
And this is a larval crab that was kind of just&nbsp;
drifting, there you can see his two eyes. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:12.720
this was a little bigger, I think at the largest&nbsp;
size you can photograph under a microscope and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:18.080
really too small for my for my lens but&nbsp;
I very much enjoyed this process and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:22.720
discovering an array of different animals&nbsp;
and I was surprised how much time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.720 --> 00:02:27.440
it took a lot of time, just like my photography.&nbsp;
It's a whole amount of looking through each slide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.440 --> 00:02:32.240
was like a journey of a day of diving&nbsp;
it was really a wonderful experience.

00:02:32.240 --> 00:02:37.600
And then I decided to try and collaborate with&nbsp;
a friend to go even smaller and this is using a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:44.560
scanning electron microscope, and this is a diatom&nbsp;
so you can see this is extremely tiny here we're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.560 --> 00:02:49.120
at like one micrometer, and so it's just the&nbsp;
magnification is twenty thousand times it's,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.120 --> 00:02:52.720
you know we're right in there but this is very&nbsp;
different because it's not with the light and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.720 --> 00:02:57.200
you use your positioning and the focus is very&nbsp;
different I don't know really how to explain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.200 --> 00:03:03.920
it other than it's a lot of like controls and&nbsp;
looking on a screen and very different to how I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.920 --> 00:03:09.600
do it, and again the same search for how to find&nbsp;
wildlife and how to find the right diatom are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:14.160
not all are the same some don't line up the right&nbsp;
way and it's all the same problems of photography&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:17.840
just at this minute scale. But I very&nbsp;
much enjoyed the process of learning.

